@page "/exsheet"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Sheet

<DocsPage Product="ExSheet" Title="Overview"
          Lead="A general-purpose sheet for Blazor: cells addressed A1 over Excel's full extent, Formulas computed as Excel computes them, a Formula Bar and a Name Box. ExSheet holds and computes; one ExGrid paints it.">

    <DocsSection Title="Responsive sizing">
        <p>
            ExSheet draws its cells with ExGrid. A <code>ViewportWidth="ViewportSize.Stretch"</code>
            follows the available box while keeping column widths and the two-dimensional grid;
            a narrower box is reached by horizontal scrolling. Enclosing flex and grid items must
            permit shrinking with <code>min-width: 0</code>. A <code>MudExGridPaper</code> does so itself.
            See the <a href="exgrid#a-responsive-box">responsive grid Example</a> for both Chromes.
            This sizing support does not yet claim the complete mobile touch workflow.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What ExSheet is">
        <p>
            ExSheet holds a <b>Sheet</b> — 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns, stored sparsely — and computes
            the <b>Value</b> of every cell from its <b>Entry</b>, what the user typed. It shows the Sheet by
            rendering one ExGrid as that grid's Consumer: ExSheet holds and computes, ExGrid paints, selects,
            navigates and reports. Everything you learn about ExGrid's selection, keyboard and clipboard
            holds inside a Sheet.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>The Sheet Document is yours to keep.</b> After every change ExSheet raises
                <code>DocumentChanged</code> with a new <code>SheetDocument</code>, and stores nothing itself.
                Bind it two ways and persist it wherever your application keeps its data.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A document holds Entries, never Values.</b> <code>=B2*C2</code> is saved as the Formula, not as
                the number it gave. Anyone who wants a saved Sheet's numbers runs the engine,
                <code>ExSheet.Engine</code>, which gives the same result on a server as in the browser.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>It answers as Excel does, or says it cannot.</b> A function Excel has that ExSheet does not support
                is <code>#NAME?</code>, which no Formula catches, a name Excel does not have is Excel's own
                <code>#NAME?</code>, a circular reference is <code>#CIRC!</code>, and a number that does not fit its
                column shows <code>####</code> — never an approximation.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A cell takes what is typed, as Excel's does.</b> ExSheet asks no verdict of yours before an Entry
                is written, so the grid's validation, and a verdict answered later, are not offered on a Sheet. A rule
                over your data is checked on the document <code>DocumentChanged</code> hands you.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            ExSheet ships in three packages: <code>ExSheet</code> (the component), <code>ExSheet.Engine</code>
            (the Formula engine, with no UI and no dependency) and <code>ExSheet.MudBlazor</code> (Format Cells
            as a <code>MudDialog</code>, and ExGrid.MudBlazor's controls in the grid). ExSheet is a prerelease:
            its API may change between versions.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under React it is <code>@@ex-grid/sheet-react</code>, and <code>@@ex-grid/sheet-mantine</code> (published
            with 0.3.0) draws it with Mantine: <code>MantineExSheet</code> takes an ExSheet's props, stands the Sheet in
            Mantine's <code>Paper</code> (<code>withBorder</code>, <code>radius</code>, <code>shadow</code>), and draws the
            Sheet Toolbar, Format Cells, the Sheet Tabs and every part of the grid inside in Mantine's components. It needs
            a <code>MantineProvider</code> above it, and <code>@@ex-grid/mantine/styles.css</code> and
            <code>@@ex-grid/sheet-mantine/styles.css</code> loaded after Mantine's styles. The Chrome follows the theme
            and its scheme; the cells keep the document's own font, Paper and Ink, which are the Workbook's, not the
            theme's, and the column widths counted in its characters. A part you give in <code>chrome</code> beats
            Mantine's, and <code>mantineSheetChrome</code> is the Wrapper's parts alone.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>@@ex-grid/sheet-mui</code> (published with 0.3.0) draws it with MUI the same way:
            <code>MuiExSheet</code> takes an ExSheet's props and stands the Sheet in MUI's <code>Paper</code>
            (<code>variant</code>, outlined unless you say <code>elevation</code>, <code>elevation</code> and
            <code>square</code>). It needs a <code>ThemeProvider</code> above it whose theme is made with
            <code>createTheme(&#123; cssVariables: true &#125;)</code>, and <code>@@ex-grid/mui/styles.css</code> and
            <code>@@ex-grid/sheet-mui/styles.css</code> loaded; a theme without CSS variables, or with a
            <code>cssVarPrefix</code> of its own, is refused by name, because the Chrome would otherwise be drawn in
            colours that are not the theme's. As under Mantine, the cells keep the document's font, Paper and Ink, a part
            you give in <code>chrome</code> beats MUI's, and <code>muiSheetChrome</code> is the Wrapper's parts alone.
            Switch the Example below to Mantine or MUI to see either.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A first sheet">
        <p>
            Bind <code>Document</code> to a field. A null document is an empty Sheet in <code>Culture</code>
            (the current culture when you leave it out); a document carries its own culture, which is how typed
            constants are read and Values are shown. Click a cell and read its Entry in the Formula Bar, press
            F2 on a Formula to edit it, type an address such as <code>D200</code> into the Name Box, and use
            Ctrl+arrow to jump to the end of a block.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FirstSheet)" Mud="typeof(FirstSheetMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/Expenses.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
        <p>
            Beyond typing, the user gets what a spreadsheet user expects: the fill handle (Formulas with
            their References shifted, a series from two or more numbers, dates by day, and the one number of a
            source that holds nothing else going on by one, the blanks beside it left blank, as Excel's does —
            any other pattern is refused, with the reason; for whole columns or rows it stands beside the
            Heading, and from one whole column or row it fills every row or column, a single number going on by
            one, as Excel's did beside it in the readings of 2026-10-12, R5), Ctrl+D and Ctrl+R, Delete to clear contents and keep formats, Ctrl+F to
            find text as it is shown and Ctrl+H to replace an Entry's text, within the Sheet or the whole Workbook
            (<a href="exsheet/workbook#find-and-replace">Find and Replace</a>), and a Context Menu that inserts and deletes the rows or columns the
            Selection spans — as Ctrl++ and Ctrl+- do from the keyboard, on a Selection of whole rows or whole
            columns. References keep naming the same cells, and a deleted target becomes
            <code>#REF!</code>.
            A menu key typed immediately after right-click waits for that Context Menu to take the
            keyboard, under either Chrome and on Blazor Server too.
            The menu keeps the pressed cell even if committing an edit moves the Sheet on the page.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>PinnedColumnCount</code> freezes leading columns.</li>
            <li><code>ShowRowHeadings</code>, <code>ShowColumnHeadings</code> and <code>ShowFormulaBar</code> hide the Headings or the Formula Bar; the cells are still addressed A1.</li>
            <li>For a screen reader the Column Headings are the table's row 1, so the grid numbers a Sheet row as its number plus one, near the top and far down: A1 is in row 2. With <code>ShowColumnHeadings</code> off, the two numbers agree.</li>
            <li>
                A copy carries the Values as shown in <code>text/plain</code> and unformatted in
                <code>text/html</code>. A paste may spill from one cell, as in Excel, and each pasted field is
                read as if typed under the Sheet's culture.
            </li>
            <li>
                A Sheet taller than the window scrolls the page with the Focus: an arrow, Enter or Tab, Ctrl+arrow,
                and an edit opening bring the cell into the window at the nearest edge, as
                <a href="exgrid/selection#the-page-follows-the-focus">ExGrid's moves do</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                A copy or a paste the grid refuses — a block that would run past XFD1048576, say — is announced once,
                by the grid's live region, in the Sheet's own words, and the Sheet's notice under the grid shows the
                same sentence. <code>CommandLabel</code> replaces those words as it replaces the menu's, by the ids in
                ExGrid's <a href="exgrid/clipboard#what-a-refusal-sounds-like"><code>RefusalLabelIds</code></a>;
                under React, <code>commandLabel</code> words the grid's ids and <code>label</code> the Sheet's own.
                The Context Menu offers Copy with headers only when <code>AllowCopyWithHeaders</code>
                (<code>allowCopyWithHeaders</code>) is on: a Sheet's column letters are addresses, not headers.
                A large paste on Blazor Server says it is under way while it is read, and <kbd>Escape</kbd> then
                cancels it, as <a href="exgrid/clipboard#a-paste-under-way-and-escape">ExGrid's does</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A sheet drawn in the browser">
        <p>
            Blazor's ExSheet is moving onto the same TypeScript core as React's, as ExGrid and ExPivot are.
            <code>ExSheet.Script.ExSheet</code> is that Sheet, beside the one above while the move is under way: the
            Workbook, its Formulas, the Formula Bar, the Sheet Tabs and the status-bar figures run in the browser on
            both hosting models, so on Blazor Server a key typed into a cell no longer crosses the circuit. It takes
            the parameters above under the same names and defaults, so a page moves to it by its type. The documents
            cross as the JSON both engines read and write: <code>Document</code> or <code>Workbook</code> goes to the
            browser when you hand over one of your own, and each change comes back through <code>DocumentChanged</code>
            and <code>WorkbookChanged</code>; one it raised, bound back two ways, crosses no more.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreSheet)" Mud="typeof(ScriptCoreSheetMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/Expenses.cs"])" />
        <p>
            It carries <code>Document</code>, <code>Workbook</code> and their events, <code>SheetsChanged</code>,
            <code>Culture</code>, <code>PinnedColumnCount</code>, <code>RowHeight</code>, the sizes, the Headings,
            the Formula Bar, the Sheet Tabs, <code>ShowToolbar</code> with Excel's default row, the status-bar
            figures' <code>SummaryFigures</code>, <code>ShowSelectionSummary</code> and <code>SummaryFiguresChanged</code>,
            <code>SelectionChanged</code>, <code>AllowCopyWithHeaders</code>, <code>EditingChanged</code> and
            <code>IsEditing</code>, <code>OnFormatKeyRefused</code>, <code>Seed</code>, <code>Today</code>,
            <code>TimeZone</code> and <code>CommandLabel</code>, which is asked for every id once each time the
            parameters are set, its words crossing only when they changed. <code>UndoAsync</code>,
            <code>RedoAsync</code>, <code>OpenFormatCellsAsync</code>, <code>DoAsync</code>,
            <code>SetCellFormatAsync</code>, <code>SetNumberFormatAsync</code>, <code>SetAlignmentAsync</code>,
            <code>DeclareLinkedTableAsync</code> and <code>PushLinkedTableAsync</code> are asked of the Sheet in the
            browser and throw what it refused or threw as the Sheet above does: a <code>SheetEdit</code> crosses as
            <code>SheetWire</code> writes it, the text the TypeScript engine reads. What the Sheet above answers at
            once is asked here, so it is awaited: <code>CellFormatAtAsync</code> and <code>LinkedTablesAsync</code>
            read the Sheet in the browser, and its documents are read with <code>ToDocumentAsync</code> and
            <code>ToWorkbookDocumentAsync</code>. <code>OpenAsync</code> and <code>SaveAsync</code> take their place
            among the Sheet's inputs in the browser, as the Sheet above's do, and call back into .NET in their turn:
            your read runs in .NET, and an Escape that cancels the opening cancels its token; your write is handed
            .NET's own copy of the browser's Workbook, made from its document, its Linked Tables' rows, its seed and
            its day, so that it computes the Values the browser shows. <code>OnSelectionSummaryChanged</code> hands
            you the Selection Summary the Sheet in the browser answered, as the Sheet above's does. A document, or
            anything else the browser hands .NET, that is larger than a message of a Server circuit carries crosses as
            a stream, so the hub's 32 KB limit need not be raised for it. A <code>PointingScope</code> and
            <code>OnPointingRefused</code> point into <code>ExGrid.Script.ExGrid</code>s registered in the Scope
            (<a href="exsheet/linked-tables">Linked Tables</a> says how). Its <code>Chrome</code> fills the slots of
            the Sheet's grid as <a href="exgrid#a-grid-drawn-in-the-browser">ExGrid's</a> does: a part drawn in Razor,
            such as <code>MudSheetChrome</code>'s Context Menu, is rendered beside the Sheet and moved into its place
            by the script, so the MudBlazor switch above shows the Sheet in MudBlazor's menu. The find panel stays the
            Sheet's own, with Within, as it does above under a Chrome that draws none. Not carried yet: the Sheet's
            own slots of an <code>ISheetChrome</code> (its Toolbar Items, Format Cells, the Sheet Tabs' faces and its
            find panel), <code>ToolbarContent</code>, <code>OnLinkedColumnColoursChanged</code>
            and <code>SetCellFormatFromFocusAsync</code>; use the Sheet above
            for those until they arrive. Its engine holds the data of
            five cultures, en-US, en-GB, de-DE, ja-JP and fr-FR, and the invariant culture: a Sheet under another is
            refused by name when it opens, where the Sheet above takes any. The script it runs is bundled
            into the package, at an address that changes with its content.
        </p>
        <p>
            The grid core under the Sheet is the Shared Script the ExGrid package ships,
            <code>_content/ExGrid/ex-grid-script.min.js</code>, which every product over the core on the page shares,
            so a page with a grid and a sheet loads it once. A Content Security Policy that lists the packages' files
            names it too.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The parts of a sheet">
        <p>
            These pages and the API name the parts of a sheet as the list under the figure does. A cell holds an
            Entry, what the user typed, and shows its Value, what the Entry evaluates to, in the cell's Number
            Format. While a Formula is edited, the Cell Editor over the cell and the Formula Bar show the same
            text, the Name Box says where the Focus is, and a Reference Outline marks the cells each Reference
            names. An error is a Value too, carried into every Formula that uses it. The Sheet Toolbar shows only
            when you ask for it. The Sheet Tabs under the grid show a tab for each Sheet of the
            <a href="exsheet/workbook">Workbook</a>, one tab while it holds one Sheet; <code>ShowSheetTabs="false"</code>
            hides them.
        </p>
        <p>
            Typing a Formula and immediately pressing a referenced cell keeps the inserted Reference,
            including over a Server round trip. Releasing that press does not restore the earlier text;
            typing again replaces it normally. Pressing the cell being typed into, just as immediately, keeps
            the edit open: the press is taken as one into the editor, which a Server round trip may not have drawn yet.
        </p>
        <DocsFigure Name="sheet-anatomy">
            An expenses claim beside last quarter's, with the total's Formula opened by F2. A script draws the figure
            from <a href="figure/sheet-anatomy" target="_blank">the sheet on a page of its own</a>.
        </DocsFigure>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Saving and opening a document">
        <p>
            <code>SheetDocument.ToJson()</code> and <code>SheetDocument.FromJson(json)</code> are its serialised
            form. A document records Entries, Cell Formats, column widths and Linked Table declarations, under a
            version number; a newer engine reads every older version, and an unknown version is refused with a
            <code>SheetDocumentException</code> rather than half-read. Under React the same is
            <code>toJson()</code> and <code>WorkbookDocument.fromJson(json)</code>, refusing with a
            <code>SheetDocumentError</code>; the document is the <code>document</code> prop, each change comes back
            through <code>onWorkbookChanged</code>, and <code>undefined</code> in its place opens an empty Sheet in
            <code>locale</code>. A workbook from Excel opens through
            <code>OpenAsync</code> and saves through <code>SaveAsync</code>, each in its place among the user's
            keys: see <a href="exsheet/xlsx">Open and save .xlsx</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Handing over a different document replaces the Sheet and clears its undo stack. If the user has an
            edit open at that moment, it is discarded and the Sheet says so, rather than typing into the new
            document — unless the new document is your answer to that edit's own commit, handed in as you hear
            <code>DocumentChanged</code>: the entry was made, in the document it was typed in, and the edit ends as
            committed. The Selection goes with the old document, and a paste, <kbd>Delete</kbd> or fill the user
            aimed at the old one that lands after the new one opened is refused, and the Sheet says another
            document was opened and nothing was written: its cells stand at the old one's places, and the write
            was meant for those. Typing aimed at the old one that reaches the Sheet after the new one opened
            opens no edit, and the Sheet says so the same way. Handing back the document ExSheet just raised — as <code>@@bind-Document</code>
            does — changes nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
            A Sheet is ExGrid's Consumer, so its edits follow the grid's rules for writes
            (<a href="exgrid/editing#editing-while-values-change">Editing while values change</a>): what the user
            enters lands as entered, and if a cell changed under the open editor — a Linked Table's snapshot
            recalculating it, say — the commit still lands, and the Sheet's notice says what it showed when
            editing began and what was written over.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(SaveAndLoad)" Mud="typeof(SaveAndLoadMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/Expenses.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Undo, and commands of your own">
        <p>
            Each ExSheet has one undo stack. Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y work from the keyboard; <code>UndoAsync</code>
            and <code>RedoAsync</code> do the same from your buttons. A command you give —
            <code>DoAsync(SheetEdit)</code>, <code>SetCellFormatAsync</code> — goes onto the same stack in its
            place in the order, so the user can undo it like anything they typed. Under React they are on the
            component's ref: <code>undo()</code>, <code>redo()</code>, <code>do(edit)</code> and
            <code>setCellFormat</code>, with <code>canUndo</code> and <code>canRedo</code>. An undo or a redo selects the
            range its step changed, as Excel's does — the cell typed in, not the one <kbd>Enter</kbd> moved to; the
            range pasted, filled or formatted; the whole rows or columns inserted or deleted — and scrolls its
            top-left cell into view. The grid selects one range, so a step over several selects the range they make
            together when they make one rectangle, as the whole columns of one resize of B to D do, and otherwise
            the first of them in Selection order, the one it acted from: <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Enter</kbd> over a
            Selection of several ranges selects the one it was typed in, and a resize of B and D alone selects B.
            In a Workbook of several Sheets an undo shows the Sheet its step was taken on, and a command names the
            Sheet it acts on, <code>DoAsync("Claim", edit)</code>
            (<a href="exsheet/workbook#commands-name-their-sheet">Commands name their Sheet</a>).
        </p>
        <p>
            While a cell or the Formula Bar is being edited, every command is refused with a
            <code>SheetRefusedException</code> (<code>SheetRefusedError</code> under React) and changes nothing, as Excel greys out its ribbon: a row inserted
            above the cell would otherwise carry the typing into another row. <code>EditingChanged</code>
            (<code>onEditingChanged</code>) tells you when an edit opens and closes, so your buttons can grey out too. Commands act on the Selection
            as the grid holds it when they run.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(UndoRedo)" Mud="typeof(UndoRedoMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/Expenses.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Status-bar figures">
        <p>
            Select cells and the line under the Sheet shows Excel's Average, Count and Sum; right-click it to
            choose among all six. The Sheet answers from its own cells: a Formula's value, never its text, and
            every cell an array spills into. A date is a number here, as in Excel. An error among the cells
            leaves Count and Numerical Count, and no other figure, as in Excel. A cell whose Formula ExSheet refused
            leaves no figure at all, and the line says why, naming the cell;
            <code>SheetCommandIds.SummaryRefused</code> words it in another language, asked of a substituted
            Chrome and then of <code>CommandLabel</code>, its <code>{0}</code> the cell and <code>{1}</code> what it shows. Average, Min, Max and Sum wear the
            active cell's Number Format, whatever the other cells' formats. <code>ShowSelectionSummary="false"</code>
            turns the line off. <code>SummaryFigures</code> chooses the figures; the Sheet keeps the user's choice from
            the menu until you hand it other figures, and <code>SummaryFiguresChanged</code> hears the choice.
            Under React these are <code>showSelectionSummary</code>, <code>summaryFigures</code>,
            <code>onSummaryFiguresChanged</code> and <code>onSelectionSummaryChanged</code>, and the reason is worded
            through <code>label</code>'s <code>summaryRefused</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Driving a sheet from a script">
        <p>
            The grid a Sheet is drawn with carries the <MudLink Href="exgrid/selection">Settled Mark</MudLink>:
            <code>data-ex-settled</code> on its root once every key, press on the cells, paste and copy it has
            taken has been handled and painted. A test or a macro types at full speed, waits for the mark, and
            reads. Text typed into the Cell Editor, Formula Bar and Name Box is covered too: the next
            grid command takes the full field text, and the mark waits for its rendered answer.
            A press on the Sheet Toolbar still needs an expectation that waits for its effect.
        </p>
        <p>
            On Blazor Server, input made while the connection is lost never reaches the Sheet. Once the connection is
            back, the next input finds it missing: it is refused aloud and never replayed, and the new input is handled
            as usual, as on <MudLink Href="exgrid/selection">the grid</MudLink>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="See it in an application">
        <p>
            The <MudLink Href="showcase/budget" Target="_blank">budget sheet showcase</MudLink> is a departmental
            budget as a finance team would build it: months across, cost lines down, subtotals and a variance
            column, number formats, and the Sheet Toolbar.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

